<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cavtat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epidaurum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cavtat is a small town on the Adriatic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, on the site of the Greek and Roman town of Epidaurum. According to tradition, refugees from Epidaurum founded Ragusa (Dubrovnik) after the town was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the seventh century. Later part of the Republic of Ragusa, it is known for the Račić family mausoleum by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a> and as the birthplace of the painter Vlaho Bukovac.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cavtat.jpg" alt="Cavtat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavtat.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cavtat lies on the Adriatic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, in Dubrovnik-Neretva County, 15 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. It is the centre and main settlement of the municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konavle">Konavle</a>. Its Italian name was Ragusa Vecchia, meaning Old Ragusa, and the modern Croatian name derives from the Latin Civitas Vetus, old city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek settlers from Corinth founded the city in the 6th century BCE as Epidaurus, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> of the surrounding area called it Zaptal. It became Epidaurum when it came under Roman rule in 228 BCE. During the Gothic War of 535–554 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> sent his fleet to the town and occupied it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> sacked and destroyed the city in the 7th century. Refugees from Epidaurum fled to the nearby island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laus">Laus</a>, called Ragusa, which in time grew into the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. The town was re-established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as Ragusa Vecchia and after a short time came under the control of the Republic of Ragusa, its powerful neighbour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cavtat is a tourist destination with hotels, rented rooms, shops and restaurants along its seafront, and a ferry connects it with Mlini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. The town cemetery on the hill holds the mausoleum of the Račić family, decorated by the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a>. The Epidaurus Festival of Music has been held there every year since 2007. Natives of the town include the painter Vlaho Bukovac (1855–1922), the jurist Baltazar Bogišić (1834–1908) and the politician Frano Supilo (1870–1917).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cavtat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…off the southern Dalmatian coast; according to tradition it was founded in the seventh century by refugees from the Roman town of <b>Epidaurum</b> (modern Cavtat), destroyed by Avars and Slavs, and a Slavic settlement grew up on the mainland opposite, the channel between them later being filled in.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
